CHAPTERS = [
dict(title="Winter in a Viking Longhouse", scene="longhouse", paras=[
"Good evening, and welcome to The Quiet Archive. This is a place for slow stories and soft voices, and tonight there is nothing at all you need to do. You do not have to follow every word. If your thoughts wander away, let them go, and if sleep arrives before the end, that is exactly what this story is for.",

"So settle in. Pull the blanket a little higher. Let your jaw unclench, and your forehead smooth out, and let one long breath leave you, slowly, like the last thread of smoke rising from a fire that is almost asleep.",
"Tonight we are travelling north, about a thousand years back in time, to a farm at the head of a Norwegian fjord, in the deepest part of winter. Snow lies over everything. The days are short and pale, and the nights are very long. And at the centre of the farm stands one long, low house, with a fire burning inside it.",
"We will spend the whole of one winter evening in that house, with the family and the animals and the fire. Nothing much will happen. And that is the point.",
]),

dict(title="The Farm at the Head of the Fjord", scene="fjord", paras=[
"Imagine a long arm of the sea, reaching far inland between steep mountains. The water is dark and very still, and the mountains rise straight out of it, white with snow, so high that in midwinter the sun barely clears their tops. This is a fjord. The western coast of Norway is cut by dozens of them, some running more than a hundred miles into the land.",
"At the very end of this fjord, where a river comes down from the hills, there is a small patch of flat ground. In summer it is green, with meadows for hay and a few strips of barley. Now it is buried under a smooth, deep layer of snow, broken only by a line of footprints leading from the shore up to the house.",
"A farm like this was never just one building. Around the long house there are several smaller ones, scattered across the snow. A storehouse raised up on stones, to keep the food dry and away from mice. A low workshop half dug into the ground, where the iron tools are mended. A shed for the sheep. Each roof is a soft white hump, and each is joined to the others by narrow paths, trodden flat in the snow.",
"The people who live here are one large household. A farmer and his wife, their children, an old grandmother who has seen more winters than anyone else in the valley, and two or three workers who help with the animals and the fields. Through the dark months, they will all live, and eat, and sleep together, in the one long room around the fire.",
"The people who lived here a thousand years ago did not divide the year into four seasons the way we do. In the old Norse way of reckoning, there were really only two. Summer, and winter. Winter began in the middle of autumn, with a time called the winter nights, and from then on, for half the year, life drew inward, toward the house and the fire.",
"Their months had names that tell you a good deal about life in the cold. In Iceland, where these old names were remembered longest, the month from late October to late November was called Gormánuður, the slaughter month, when animals that could not be fed through the winter were killed, and their meat was salted, smoked, or dried. The month after that, the month we are in tonight, was called Ýlir, and it led up to the great midwinter feast of Yule.",
"Down by the shore, a small wooden boat has been pulled high up onto the stones and turned over, so that the snow slides off its curved planks. Beside it, there is a long boathouse, half sunk into the ground, with a roof of turf. Inside, the farm's larger boat waits out the winter, its oars stacked neatly along the wall.",
"The air is so cold and so still that the smoke from the house rises in a straight grey column, without bending at all, until it fades into the darkening sky. Somewhere across the water, a raven calls once, a deep, hollow sound, and the mountains send it back, softer, a moment later.",
"It is only the middle of the afternoon. But already the light is going. The snow on the high peaks turns faintly pink, then lilac, then grey. And down below, in the long house at the head of the fjord, someone is adding wood to the fire.",
]),

dict(title="Inside the Longhouse", scene="hearth", paras=[
"Let us walk up the path through the snow, toward the house. It is long and low, perhaps twenty paces from end to end, with walls that curve gently outward in the middle, like the side of a boat. The roof is thick with turf and covered in snow, so that from a distance the whole house looks like a long white hill, with smoke rising from its spine.",
"The door is small, and you have to stoop a little to go in. Inside there is a narrow entrance room, and then a second door. This was a simple and clever way of keeping the warmth in. When the outer door opened, the cold air stopped in the little room, and never reached the fire.",
"And then you are inside. It takes a moment for your eyes to adjust. There are no windows here, or almost none. The only light comes from the fire, and from a few small lamps, and from a square hole in the roof high above, where the smoke drifts slowly out into the night.",
"The house is divided by two long rows of wooden posts, running the length of the room, thick tree trunks holding up the great weight of the roof. They make three aisles, like the inside of a church. Down the central aisle, the floor is beaten earth, dry and hard, scattered with ash to soak up the damp.",
"In the middle of that floor is the long hearth. It is a low, rectangular bed of stones, set into the ground, several paces long. A fire of birch logs burns along it, not roaring, just steady, glowing orange and red. Its warmth reaches out to both sides of the house, and its light flickers up along the posts and into the smoky shadows of the roof.",
"Along both walls, on either side of the fire, there are wide wooden platforms, raised a little off the floor, and running almost the whole length of the house. These were the benches, and they were the heart of daily life. People sat on them to eat, and worked on them through the day, and at night, they spread out furs and woollen blankets, and slept on them.",
"Only the farmer and his wife had a proper bed of their own, in a small closed space at one end of the room, like a wooden cupboard with a door, lined with straw and sheepskins. Everyone else slept on the benches. And there was very little other furniture at all. A few wooden chests stood along the walls, and the low tables used for meals were simply lifted up and hung from the roof beams when they were not needed.",
"The woman of the house has a bunch of keys hanging from a brooch at her shoulder. They open the chests, and the storehouse, and the pantry. In the old Norse world, the keys were a sign of her authority. The house, and everything stored in it to carry the household through the winter, was hers to manage, and she knew to the last handful how much was left.",
"Here and there along the benches, small lamps are burning. Some are shallow bowls carved from soapstone, a soft grey stone that was easy to shape. They are filled with oil made from fish livers, or from seals, and the wick is a little twist of fluffy cotton grass gathered from the bogs in summer. The flame is small and steady, and gives just enough light to work by.",
"The air smells of woodsmoke and wool, of drying fish and hay and warm animals. It is not a perfectly clean smell. But it is a deeply comforting one. It is the smell of a house that is ready for winter.",
]),

dict(title="The Animals in the Byre", scene="longhouse", paras=[
"At the far end of the house, beyond a wooden partition, you can hear soft sounds in the dark. A long, contented breath. The shifting of hooves on straw. The slow, steady grinding of jaws. This end of the building is the byre, and in many longhouses of this time, the cattle lived under the same roof as the family through the winter.",
"There was good sense in it. The animals were safe from the cold and the wolves, and they were easy to feed and milk without anyone having to go out into the snow. And their bodies gave off a gentle warmth of their own, which drifted through the house and helped the fire keep out the frost.",
"There are only a few cows here. They are small and sturdy, much smaller than modern cattle, with shaggy winter coats. Each stands in its own narrow stall, divided by stone slabs or wooden boards set upright in the floor. One of them turns her head as you come near, looks at you calmly with large dark eyes, and goes back to her hay.",
"In the stall nearest the door stands a small horse, with a thick, rough coat and a long, untidy mane. Horses like this were tough and sure footed, and could carry a rider or a load along the steep paths of the fjord in any weather. Tonight, he dozes on his feet, one back hoof tipped up and resting, his lower lip drooping, his eyes half closed.",
"That hay was the most precious thing on the farm. All through the short summer, the family had cut grass on the meadows and on the steep slopes above the fjord, and dried it, and carried it home in great bundles. Every evening now, someone measures out a careful armful for each animal. The whole question of the winter was whether the hay would last until the grass grew again.",
"The sheep are kept nearby, in a low turf shed of their own, close against the wall of the house. They are a hardy, short tailed northern breed, with thick fleeces of many colours. White, and grey, and brown, and nearly black. Through the winter they huddle together in the straw, a single warm, woolly mass, breathing slow clouds into the cold air.",
"A girl comes through the partition with a wooden pail. She sits on a low stool beside one of the cows, leans her forehead against its warm flank, and begins to milk. The sound is soft and rhythmic, a thin ringing at first, and then a deeper, gentler sound as the pail fills. The cow does not seem to mind at all. She has done this every evening of her life.",
"Before the household goes to sleep, someone will take a small lamp and walk the length of the byre one more time. To see that each animal is settled, that the water troughs have not frozen, that nothing is wrong. It is a quiet, unhurried walk, past the warm, breathing shapes in the dark, and it ends the day.",
"Some of that milk will be drunk fresh tonight. Most of it will be turned into butter, and cheese, and skyr, a thick, tangy kind of soured milk that was eaten in the north for centuries, and still is today. In the cold months, what the animals gave, and what had been stored from the summer, was all there was. And so nothing at all was wasted.",
]),

dict(title="Supper by the Fire", scene="hearth", paras=[
"Back in the main room, supper is being prepared. Over one end of the long hearth, an iron cauldron hangs from a chain, fixed to a beam high above. Inside it, something is bubbling slowly. A thick porridge of barley, cooked in water with a little milk, and stirred now and then with a long wooden spoon.",
"Beside the fire, a flat stone has been heating in the embers. A woman takes a handful of dough, made from barley flour and water, and presses it out into a thin round with her palms. She lays it on the hot stone. It puffs and blisters and browns in only a moment, and then she turns it over. Flat bread like this was baked fresh almost every day.",
"From the rafters, in the smoky air near the roof, hang the stores of winter. Strips of meat and sides of mutton, slowly drying and taking on the taste of the smoke. Bundles of herbs. And rows of fish, split open and dried hard in the cold wind of early spring, as stiff and light as pieces of wood. Before they are eaten, they are soaked, and then beaten with a stone to soften them.",
"Water for the cooking comes from the river. Earlier, one of the boys walked down with an axe and two wooden buckets, and cut a hole through the ice near the bank, where the water still runs fast and clear underneath. He carried the buckets back up the path, slowly, steam rising from the water in the cold air, and set them down by the hearth, where the thin skin of ice on top has already melted away.",
"Remember the name of the month that comes after this one. Mörsugur, which means something like the fat sucking month. By then, the fresh food of autumn was gone, and people lived on what they had laid by. Butter and lard, and dried meat, and the rich marrow from the bones. It was a name that made people smile, the way we might joke about the long, lean weeks after a holiday.",
"Now the family gathers along the benches. Everyone has their own wooden bowl, and their own spoon, carved from wood or from horn, and their own small knife, worn at the belt. The porridge is ladled out, steaming. There is bread, and butter, and a little dried fish, and a lump of soft white cheese. For a while, nobody says very much at all.",
"To drink, there is ale, brewed on the farm from barley, and served in wooden cups. It is mild and a little cloudy, with a faint sweetness. On a special evening, there might be mead, made from honey, but honey was rare this far north, and mead was kept for feasts and for honoured guests.",
"The bowls are scraped clean. The fire is fed a few more pieces of birch, and the bark curls and crackles and flares. Outside, the wind has risen a little, and you can hear it moving over the roof, a long, low sigh, like the sea. But in here, it cannot reach you. In here, there is only the fire, and the warmth, and the slow end of a meal.",
"The dog, who has been lying very patiently beside the hearth through the whole meal, is given a bone. He carries it off to a corner under the bench, and you can hear him there in the shadows for a long time afterward, gnawing contentedly, and then falling quiet.",
]),

dict(title="Handwork in the Lamplight", scene="hearth", paras=[
"After supper, nobody goes to bed. The winter evening is long, and there is always work for the hands. But it is a quiet, sitting kind of work, the kind that lets the mind wander, and leaves room for talk.",
"Near the lamps, one of the women is spinning. In her left hand she holds a staff with a soft cloud of combed wool tied to the top. With her right hand she draws out a few fibres and twists them, and lets a little wooden spindle drop toward the floor, spinning as it falls. The spindle has a round weight near its base, called a whorl, made of soapstone or clay, which keeps it turning smoothly. The thread grows longer, and longer, and is wound onto the spindle, and the spindle drops again.",
"Every thread that the household wore was made this way, by hand. Every shirt, every dress, every cloak and blanket. And above all, the sails. A single large sail for a sea going ship took an enormous amount of wool, and many months, even years, of patient spinning and weaving. Without this quiet work by the fire, there would have been no ships at all.",
"Against the wall stands the loom. It is tall and upright, leaning back slightly against the wall, and the threads hang straight down from a beam at the top. At the bottom, they are held tight by rows of heavy weights, made of baked clay, each one shaped like a small, flat ring. Archaeologists find these loom weights in great numbers, often lying in a neat row on the floor, exactly where an old loom once stood.",
"The weaver works standing up. She passes the thread back and forth through the hanging warp, and then beats it upward with a long, smooth sword of wood or whalebone, so that the cloth grows from the top downward. Each pass makes a soft, muffled knock. It is a slow, steady rhythm, and it fills the house like a quiet heartbeat.",
"On the other bench, a man is carving. He holds a block of birch wood in one hand and a small knife in the other, and long, pale curls of wood fall into his lap. He is making a new spoon. When it is finished, he will carve a little pattern into the handle, two creatures twisting around each other, their bodies looping and interlacing, in the style the Norse people loved.",
"Some of the wool on the benches has been dyed. A soft blue, from the leaves of a plant called woad. A warm red, from the roots of madder, which had to be traded from further south. Yellows and greens from the plants of the meadows. Most everyday clothing was left in the natural colours of the sheep, white and grey and brown. But a little bright colour, at the edge of a cloak or around a sleeve, was a quiet pleasure, and a sign of care.",
"An older woman is making a sock, but not in the way you might expect. She uses a single flat needle, carved from bone, with an eye at one end, and she builds the fabric up in small loops, one through another, using short lengths of yarn. This method is called nålbinding, and it is older than knitting. The fabric it makes is thick, and warm, and almost impossible to unravel.",
"For a long time, there is no sound but the work. The click of the spindle. The knock of the loom. The whisper of the knife through the wood. And the fire, murmuring to itself along the hearth.",
]),

dict(title="A Game of Tafl", scene="hearth", paras=[
"Close to the fire, two of the young men have pulled a board between them on the bench. It is a square of wood, cut with a grid of small squares, and on it stand two little armies of playing pieces, some pale and some dark. They are playing hnefatafl, the king's table, one of the favourite games of the Viking world.",
"It is an unusual game, because the two sides are not equal. One player has a king, standing on the very centre square, surrounded by a small band of defenders. The other player has a larger army, arranged around the edges of the board. The defenders are trying to lead their king safely to the edge, or into a corner, and escape. The attackers are trying to surround him and capture him.",
"Every piece moves the same way, in a straight line, as far as it likes, along the rows of the board, much like the rook in chess. A piece is taken when it is trapped between two enemy pieces. And so the game is all about patience and position. A single careless move can open a path for the king, or close one forever.",
"Nobody today knows exactly how the Vikings played it, because they never wrote the rules down. The rules most people use now come from a much later description. In the year 1732, the Swedish naturalist Carl Linnaeus, travelling in the far north, watched the Sámi people playing a similar game called tablut, and wrote down how it worked. From his notes, the old game has been gently pieced back together.",
"But the pieces themselves survive, in many places. Little rounded or cone shaped pieces made of glass, or bone, or antler, or amber. A set of glass pieces was found in a grave at Birka, in Sweden. A board for the game was found in the great Gokstad ship burial, in Norway, with a different game marked on its other side. And people seem to have been buried with their pieces, as if they hoped to keep on playing.",
"The two players are in no hurry. One of them leans back against a post, considering the board for a long time, and then moves a single dark piece, one square. The other nods slowly, and rubs his chin, and looks at the board again. Their younger sister sits beside them, watching every move, already sure that she could do better.",
"At the other end of the bench, the grandmother is asking riddles. The Norse loved these too, and in one of the old sagas, a king and a mysterious guest spend a whole evening trying to outwit each other with them. The children frown and whisper and guess. What can fill a whole house from wall to wall, and yet weighs nothing at all? What runs all night without ever leaving its bed? The answers are passed around, laughed at, and argued over.",
"The game will not be finished tonight. That does not matter. The board will be set carefully on a shelf, with the pieces left exactly where they are, ready to be taken down again tomorrow evening, and the evening after that. There are many long evenings still to come.",
]),

dict(title="Stories at Midwinter", scene="fjord", paras=[
"As the night deepens, the household draws closer around the fire. The spindle is put away, and the loom falls quiet. And someone, usually the oldest person in the house, begins to tell a story.",
"The Norse people loved stories more than almost anything. Through the long winters, they told them again and again. Stories of families and their quarrels, of voyages to distant islands, of the gods and the giants and the making of the world. For centuries, most of these tales were never written down at all. They lived only in memory, passed from one voice to the next, beside thousands of fires just like this one.",
"Many of them were finally written down in Iceland, in the twelve hundreds and the thirteen hundreds, by people who wanted to keep the old stories safe. One of those writers, Snorri Sturluson, gathered up the old tales of the gods in a book we now call the Prose Edda. And in it, there is a gentle little story about the night.",
"Snorri also wrote about the way poets spoke. A Norse poet, a skald, rarely called a thing by its plain name. Instead, he used little riddling phrases, called kennings. A ship might be called the horse of the sea. The sea itself might be the road of the whales. And gold, Snorri explains, could be called the fire of the sea, because in one old story, the hall of the sea giant was lit not by flames, but by the shining of gold.",
"The story goes that the night is a woman named Nótt, dark haired and dark eyed, like all her family. Every evening, she rides across the sky on a horse called Hrímfaxi, which means frost mane. And every morning, as the horse finishes its long journey, the foam from its bit falls down onto the earth below, and that is the dew you find on the grass at dawn.",
"Her son is Day, bright and fair, and he rides a horse called Skinfaxi, shining mane, whose mane lights up the whole sky and the earth beneath it. And so, in the old story, night and day simply take turns, riding one after the other around the world, each in their own time, without hurry, forever.",
"Outside, far above the fjord, something is happening in the sky. A faint green glow has appeared over the mountains, like a curtain of thin silk, slowly folding and unfolding. The northern lights. A Norwegian book written in the twelve hundreds, called the King's Mirror, describes them as they were seen in Greenland, shining like a great flame, and shooting up long, bright rays, and moving and changing, always shifting from place to place.",
"The light ripples silently across the stars, green and faintly violet, and then fades a little, and then brightens again. It makes no sound at all. The snow on the mountains glows faintly beneath it. And below, the fjord holds a dim reflection of it on its still, black water.",
"Inside, the story has turned to the coming feast. Yule. In a few weeks, at the darkest point of the year, there will be a great gathering here. There will be food and ale for days, and neighbours coming in from the other farms along the fjord, and promises made and toasts drunk to the returning sun. Because after Yule, every day will be a little longer than the one before. The light will begin to come back.",
]),

dict(title="The House Falls Asleep", scene="longhouse", paras=[
"The story has ended. The children are already asleep, curled together on the bench under a heavy woollen blanket, their faces lit softly by the fire. One by one, the grown ups lie down too, spreading out the furs, and pulling the blankets close.",
"Someone banks the fire for the night. They rake the glowing embers together, toward the middle of the long hearth, and cover them with a layer of ash. Under that soft grey blanket, the embers will keep their heat until morning, and with a breath and a handful of dry birch bark, the fire will wake again at dawn.",
"The lamps are put out, one after another, with a pinch of the fingers. Now the house is very dark. Only a dull red glow shows through the ash on the hearth, and the faintest grey light comes down through the smoke hole in the roof.",
"From the byre at the far end, there comes a long, slow, contented sigh, as one of the cows lies down in the straw. The sheep in their shed are a single, sleeping heap of wool. The farm dog has found the warmest place, right beside the hearth, and is lying with his nose tucked into his tail.",
"Outside, it has begun to snow again. Soft, large flakes are falling straight down through the windless air, settling on the turf roof, on the overturned boat by the shore, on the long path up from the water. They fill in the footprints, one by one, until the snow is smooth and new again, as though no one had ever walked there at all.",
"Down at the shore, the fjord is black and still. A thin rim of new ice has begun to form along the edge of the water, between the stones, delicate as glass. Above the mountains, the northern lights have faded to a faint green haze, and then to nothing, and the stars have come back, one by one, through the falling snow.",
"In the storehouse, the barrels and baskets sit in the cold dark, full of grain and dried fish and butter, enough, if it is used with care, to last until the spring. In the workshop, the forge is cold. In the boathouse, the long ship rests on its rollers, its oars stacked along the wall, dreaming, perhaps, of summer and open water.",
"The house sits quietly at the head of the fjord, a long white shape in the white snow, with a thin thread of smoke still rising from its roof. Inside, the whole household breathes slowly together in the warm dark. The people, and the animals, and the fire under its ash. All of them waiting, patiently, for the light to return.",
"You can wait with them. There is nothing more to do tonight. Let the snow keep falling. Let the embers keep their warmth. Let the long winter night hold you, the way it held that small, quiet house so long ago.",
"Thank you for spending this winter evening with The Quiet Archive. Sleep warmly. Rest deeply. And goodnight.",
]),
]
